  "minutes": 22,
  "title": {
    "en": "Raptors in flight"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Buzzards, Honey-buzzard, three harriers, Osprey and Black Kite"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Raptors are mostly seen in the air, and mostly from far away. The skill is to read the silhouette: how the wings are held (flat, in a shallow V, or in a deep V), the length of the tail, the size of the head, and how the bird flaps. Colour comes last, because the light is often behind the bird.\n\nAutumn migration is the best time. From late August to October, buzzards, harriers, Honey-buzzards, Ospreys and Black Kites move south on the same narrow routes. Coastal headlands, the Falsterbo peninsula in Sweden, the Danish islands and the Dutch and German coasts see thousands pass in a day. In winter the Hen Harrier and the Rough-legged Buzzard come south from Scandinavia, and Marsh Harriers move to the west.\n\nToday's pictures show the common plumages: adult buzzards, a female Marsh Harrier, a female Hen Harrier (the \"ringtail\") and a male Montagu's Harrier."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "dihedral"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A V-shape of the wings when seen from the front. Harriers hold their wings in a clear dihedral when gliding."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "ringtail"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A female or young harrier, with a white band across the rump above a barred tail."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "carpal patch"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A dark patch at the \"wrist\" of the underwing, where the leading edge bends. Large and black on a Rough-legged Buzzard."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "fingers"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The spread feathers at the tip of the wing in soaring raptors. Their number and shape help with identification."
      }
    }
  ],
